WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.106
Logging and diagnostics help teams support
IoT devices in the field.

2
00:00:04.186 --> 00:00:07.730
Reset reasons, fault counters, connection
state, update results,

3
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.861
health summaries, crash diagnostics,

4
00:00:09.941 --> 00:00:13.547
and telemetry can explain behavior that is
hard to reproduce in a lab.

5
00:00:13.627 --> 00:00:17.358
The challenge is to collect useful
evidence without exposing secrets,

6
00:00:17.438 --> 00:00:20.049
personal data, or excessive operational
detail.

7
00:00:20.129 --> 00:00:23.300
Logs must avoid keys, tokens, private
credential material,

8
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.058
and unnecessary personal data.

9
00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:28.496
User-controlled input should be recorded
carefully, if at all,

10
00:00:28.576 --> 00:00:31.684
because logs may leave the device, be
retained by services,

11
00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:34.562
appear in support bundles, or be shared
across teams.

12
00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:37.191
A log record should have a clear support
purpose.

13
00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:39.820
Reset reasons and fault data need clear
meaning.

14
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.636
A reset counter with no context may not
help support,

15
00:00:42.716 --> 00:00:45.016
while a full memory dump may reveal too
much.

16
00:00:45.096 --> 00:00:48.205
Bounded categories, version records,
selected counters,

17
00:00:48.285 --> 00:00:52.699
and concise state summaries often provide
a better balance for production devices.

18
00:00:52.779 --> 00:00:55.639
Debug diagnostics differ from production
telemetry.

19
00:00:55.719 --> 00:01:00.133
During development, engineers may enable
detailed traces and verbose assertions.

20
00:01:00.213 --> 00:01:03.508
In field builds, telemetry should be
bounded, privacy-aware,

21
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:04.894
and stable under stress.

22
00:01:04.974 --> 00:01:08.393
Build profiles should make the difference
visible and testable.

23
00:01:08.473 --> 00:01:11.271
Privacy expectations should shape device
evidence.

24
00:01:11.351 --> 00:01:14.770
An IoT device may observe homes,
workplaces, locations, habits,

25
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.280
or operational processes.

26
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:20.837
Diagnostics should collect what the
product needs to support reliability and safety

27
00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:25.455
while avoiding unnecessary retention of
user-sensitive or context-sensitive data.

28
00:01:25.535 --> 00:01:28.520
Operational readiness connects diagnostics
to action.

29
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.646
Support teams need runbooks, dashboards,
release notes,

30
00:01:31.726 --> 00:01:34.710
and fault categories that match what the
device records.

31
00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:39.080
A privacy-aware diagnostic plan gives
teams enough evidence to maintain devices

32
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.020
without treating every internal detail as
field data.
